[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"TypeScript Tutorial: Interfaces vs. Type Aliases"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building TypeScript applications, developers often face the dilemma of choosing between 'interface' and 'type' alias for defining object shapes and contracts. While they share many similarities, understanding their distinct behaviors ensures better type safety and code maintainability."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Objects"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Both interfaces and type aliases can be used to describe the shape of an object or a function with remarkably similar syntax."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"interface UserInterface {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

type UserType = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
};"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Interfaces are explicitly designed to model object shapes and are open, meaning they support declaration merging."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Type aliases can represent primitives, unions, tuples, and intersections, but they do not support declaration merging."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Extension and Composition"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interfaces extend other structures using the 'extends' keyword. Type aliases achieve similar composition using intersection types (&)."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"interface Animal {
  name: string;
}

interface Dog extends Animal {
  bark(): void;
}

type Feline = {
  name: string;
};

type Cat = Feline & {
  meow(): void;
};"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Declaration Merging"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"One unique feature of interfaces is declaration merging. If you define the same interface multiple times in the same scope, TypeScript automatically merges them into a single definition. Type aliases will throw a duplicate identifier error."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"interface Settings {
  theme: string;
}

interface Settings {
  fontSize: number;
}

// Resulting Settings interface has both theme and fontSize properties."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"As a general rule of thumb, use 'interface' for public API definitions or object-oriented structures that might need extension or merging. Use 'type' for unions, primitives, tuples, or complex conditional types where interfaces fall short."}]
